                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: A 15-year-old boy's mother is concerned about his frequent masturbation and brings him to the physician. The boy is in 9th grade, captain of his wrestling team, and does well in school. He appears embarrassed during the examination, which shows no abnormalities. What is the most appropriate next step in management? \nOptions: \nA) Advise the patient to stop masturbating\nB) Measure serum testosterone level\nC) Refer the patient for behavioral therapy\nD) Reassure the mother\nE) Prescribe sertraline\n\nThought process and reasoning:\n\n- Masturbation is a normal part of adolescent development and is not harmful in moderation.\n- The boy is doing well in school and is involved in extracurricular activities, which suggests that his behavior is not negatively impacting his life.\n- The physical examination shows no abnormalities, indicating that there are no medical issues that need to be addressed.\n- The boy's embarrassment and avoidance of eye contact are likely due to the sensitive nature of the topic and his mother's concern, rather than an indication of a psychological issue.\n\nGiven this information, the most appropriate next step in management would be to:\n\nD) Reassure the mother that her son's behavior is a normal part of adolescent development and is not harmful in moderation.",
