                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient has a history of fever, cough, headache, and diarrhea after returning from a vacation abroad. He also has patchy infiltrates in both lungs, mild hyponatremia, and mild elevation of AST/ALT levels. These symptoms suggest a respiratory and gastrointestinal infection.\n\nA) Can form an endospore - This characteristic is associated with bacteria like Bacillus and Clostridium species, which are not typically associated with respiratory and gastrointestinal infections in travelers.\n\nB) Has a rabbit reservoir - This characteristic is associated with the bacterium Francisella tularensis, which causes tularemia. Tularemia can present with respiratory symptoms, but it is less likely to cause gastrointestinal symptoms like diarrhea.\n\nC) Has no cell wall - This characteristic is associated with Mycoplasma species, which can cause respiratory infections like pneumonia. However, Mycoplasma is not typically associated with gastrointestinal symptoms like diarrhea.\n\nD) Is more common in parrot owners - This characteristic is associated with the bacterium Chlamydia psittaci, which causes psittacosis. Psittacosis can present with respiratory symptoms and is associated with exposure to birds, but it is less likely to cause gastrointestinal symptoms like diarrhea.\n\nE) Is transmitted by air conditioning - This characteristic is associated with the bacterium Legionella pneumophila, which causes Legionnaires' disease. Legionnaires' disease can present with respiratory symptoms like pneumonia and gastrointestinal symptoms like diarrhea. It is often associated with exposure to contaminated water sources, such as air conditioning systems, spas, and fountains.\n\nBased on the patient's symptoms and history, the most likely causative organism is associated with the characteristic \"Is transmitted by air conditioning\" (E). The patient likely has Legionnaires' disease caused by Legionella pneumophila.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "a6c814b2-8901-4eb3-ab7a-a0dd1fd5d076",
