                        "cot": "B) A type 1 error occurs when the null hypothesis is true but is rejected in error. \n\nExplanation: \nA type 1 error occurs when the null hypothesis (the hypothesis that there is no significant difference between groups) is true, but is rejected in error, meaning that the study concludes that there is a significant difference when there actually isn't. This can happen due to chance or random variation in the data. Option A is incorrect because a type 1 error does not relate to the power of the study. Option C is incorrect because a type 1 error occurs when the null hypothesis is true, not false. Option D is incorrect because a type 1 error is an alpha (\u03b1) error, not a beta (\u03b2) error. Option E is incorrect because a type 1 error is not dependent on the confidence interval of a study, but rather on the level of significance chosen by the researchers.",
                        "answers": [
