                    {
                        "id": "e10cb04a-65ab-4b17-913f-ee998966e3ae",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question to ensure we understand it correctly: If the authors of the study made a type 1 error, which statement would be true?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step:\n\nA type 1 error occurs when the null hypothesis is true, but it is rejected in error. In this study, the null hypothesis would be that there is no significant difference between the mepolizumab treatment groups and the placebo group in terms of asthma exacerbations and markers of asthma control.\n\nIf the authors encountered a type 1 error, it would mean that they incorrectly concluded that mepolizumab significantly reduced asthma exacerbations and improved markers of asthma control when, in reality, there was no significant difference between the treatment groups and the placebo group.\n\nNow, let's evaluate each answer choice:\n\nA) A type 1 error means the study is not significantly powered to detect a true difference between study groups. - Incorrect. This statement refers to a type 2 error, not a type 1 error.\n\nB) A type 1 error occurs when the null hypothesis is true but is rejected in error. - Correct. This statement accurately describes a type 1 error.\n\nC) A type 1 error occurs when the null hypothesis is false, yet is accepted in error. - Incorrect. This statement refers to a type 2 error, not a type 1 error.\n\nD) A type 1 error is a beta (\u03b2) error and is usually 0.1 or 0.2. - Incorrect. A type 1 error is an alpha (\u03b1) error, not a beta (\u03b2) error.\n\nE) A type 1 error is dependent on the confidence interval of a study. - Incorrect. A type 1 error is related to the significance level (alpha) of a study, not the confidence interval.\n\nSo, the correct answer is B) A type 1 error occurs when the null hypothesis is true but is rejected in error.",
                        "answers": [
