                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To determine the most likely transmitter of the organism responsible for this patient's illness, we need to consider the symptoms and the location of the patient's recent trip.\n\nThe patient presents with sudden drooping of the right side of her face, drooling, excessive tearing, asymmetry of facial muscles, inability to whistle or close the right eye, a circular red rash with central clearing, and decreased taste sensation. These symptoms are consistent with Lyme disease, which is caused by the bacterium Borrelia burgdorferi.\n\nThe patient was recently in northern Maine and spent most of her time outdoors. Lyme disease is known to be prevalent in the northeastern United States, including Maine.\n\nNow let's consider the possible transmitters listed in the answer choices:\n\nA) Ixodes scapularis - This is a species of tick, also known as the black-legged tick or deer tick, which is known to transmit Lyme disease in the northeastern United States.\n\nB) Tsetse fly - This insect is found in Africa and is known to transmit African trypanosomiasis, also known as sleeping sickness, which has different symptoms than those presented by the patient.\n\nC) Culicidae - This is a family of mosquitoes, which are not known to transmit Lyme disease.\n\nD) Dermacentor variabilis - This is a species of tick, also known as the American dog tick, which is known to transmit Rocky Mountain spotted fever, not Lyme disease.\n\nE) Anopheles - This is a genus of mosquitoes, which are known to transmit malaria, not Lyme disease.\n\nBased on the patient's symptoms and recent trip to northern Maine, the most likely transmitter of the organism responsible for her illness is Ixodes scapularis (A).",
