                    {
                        "id": "f390d130-1d70-45fa-b415-0d8eedfe34ef",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to consider the following information:\n\n1. The patient has a history of diabetes and poorly controlled hypertension.\n2. The patient is already taking enalapril (an ACE inhibitor) and metformin (an anti-diabetic medication).\n3. The patient's blood pressure is still high (155/90 mmHg), so the physician added another anti-hypertensive medication.\n4. One month later, the patient complains of new onset lower extremity swelling.\n\nNow, let's generate sub-questions to help us determine which medication was likely prescribed:\n\nSub-question 1: What are the mechanisms of action and indications for each of the medications listed?\n\nA) Metoprolol: A beta-blocker that decreases heart rate and contractility, used for hypertension, angina, and heart failure.\nB) Verapamil: A calcium channel blocker that relaxes blood vessels and decreases heart rate, used for hypertension, angina, and arrhythmias.\nC) Nifedipine: A calcium channel blocker that relaxes blood vessels, used for hypertension and angina.\nD) Hydrochlorothiazide: A thiazide diuretic that increases urine output and decreases blood volume, used for hypertension and edema.\nE) Spironolactone: An aldosterone antagonist that increases urine output and decreases blood volume, used for hypertension, heart failure, and edema.\n\nSub-question 2: Which of these medications is most likely to cause lower extremity swelling as a side effect?\n\nA) Metoprolol: Unlikely, as beta-blockers do not typically cause edema.\nB) Verapamil: Possible, as calcium channel blockers can cause peripheral edema.\nC) Nifedipine: Possible, as calcium channel blockers can cause peripheral edema.\nD) Hydrochlorothiazide: Unlikely, as diuretics are used to treat edema and are not expected to cause it.\nE) Spironolactone: Unlikely, as aldosterone antagonists are used to treat edema and are not expected to cause it.\n\nBased on the information above, the most likely medication prescribed to the patient that caused the new onset lower extremity swelling is either B) Verapamil or C) Nifedipine, as both are calcium channel blockers and can cause peripheral edema as a side effect.",
                        "answers": [
