                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStudent: Professor, I'm not sure how to work out this question. Could you help me?\n\nProfessor: Sure. So, we have a 45-year-old male with a history of diabetes and poorly controlled hypertension who presents for an annual check-up. He's taking enalapril and metformin. His blood pressure is 155/90 mmHg. We know that the goal blood pressure for this patient is less than 140/90 mmHg. So, his physician adds another anti-hypertensive medication to the patient\u2019s regimen.\n\nOne month later, the patient returns to the physician complaining of new onset lower extremity swelling. This is a symptom of heart failure. So, we're looking for a medication that can cause heart failure.\n\nLooking at the options, we can see that B, verapamil, is a calcium channel blocker. Calcium channel blockers can cause heart failure. So, B is the correct answer.",
