                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question to ensure we understand it correctly: What microscopic finding would be observed in a patient who has flat topped violaceous papules, thinning of nails, and onycholysis?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step, spelling out our thoughts and reasoning so anyone can verify them.\n\nFlat topped violaceous papules, thinning of nails, and onycholysis are clinical features of lichen planus, a chronic inflammatory skin condition. In lichen planus, the microscopic finding typically observed is:\n\nA) Prominent necrotic cell - This is not the characteristic finding in lichen planus.\nB) Suprabasal split - This is a feature of pemphigus vulgaris, not lichen planus.\nC) Basal cell degeneration - This is the correct answer. Lichen planus is characterized by basal cell degeneration, which is the destruction of the basal layer of the epidermis.\nD) Acantholysis - This is a feature of pemphigus, not lichen planus.\n\nTherefore, the right answer is C) Basal cell degeneration.",
