                        "cot": "A) Inhibition of cell wall synthesis: This mechanism is typically used by antibiotics such as penicillin and cephalosporins to treat bacterial infections. Dapsone does not target the cell wall, so this option is incorrect.\n\nB) Inhibition of ergosterol in cell membranes: This mechanism is used by antifungal drugs such as azoles and polyenes to treat fungal infections. Dapsone does have some antifungal activity, but it is not primarily used for this purpose. Therefore, this option is also incorrect.\n\nC) Inhibition of protein synthesis: This mechanism is used by antibiotics such as macrolides and tetracyclines to treat bacterial infections. Dapsone does not target protein synthesis, so this option is incorrect.\n\nD) Competition with PABA in folic acid synthesis: This is the correct mechanism of action for dapsone. It competes with para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) for binding to the enzyme dihydropteroate synthase, which is involved in the synthesis of folic acid. By inhibiting folic acid synthesis, dapsone can treat bacterial and fungal infections as well as have immunomodulatory effects.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Competition with PABA in folic acid synthesis.",
