                    {
                        "id": "8eb7b8d4-e86d-410a-907b-238ec2e93ff5",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nComplementary Knowledge:\nDapsone is an antibiotic drug that belongs to the sulfone class of antibiotics. It is used to treat a variety of bacterial and fungal infections, as well as for immunomodulatory actions.\n\nSub-Questions:\n1. How does dapsone work to treat bacterial and fungal infections?\n2. How does dapsone work to provide immunomodulatory actions?\n\nAnswer to Sub-Question 1:\nDapsone works to treat bacterial and fungal infections by inhibiting the synthesis of the cell wall, which is essential for the growth and survival of bacteria and fungi.\n\nAnswer to Sub-Question 2:\nDapsone works to provide immunomodulatory actions by inhibiting the synthesis of ergosterol in cell membranes, which is essential for the proper functioning of the immune system.\n\nAnswer to Original Question:\nThe mechanism of dapsone for bacterial and fungal infections and immunomodulatory actions is inhibition of cell wall synthesis and inhibition of ergosterol in cell membranes, respectively. The correct answer is A) Inhibition of cell wall synthesis and B) Inhibition of ergosterol in cell membranes.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
