                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nThe Bezold-Jarisch reflex is a reflex that is triggered by stimulation of the cardiac vagal afferents in the heart. This reflex is responsible for the bradycardia (slowing of the heart rate) that is seen in response to certain drugs, such as morphine.\n\nThe reflex is mediated by the release of a neurotransmitter called acetylcholine from the vagal afferents. Acetylcholine binds to muscarinic receptors on the sinoatrial node, which is the pacemaker of the heart. This binding causes a decrease in the rate of spontaneous depolarization of the sinoatrial node, resulting in a decrease in heart rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Prostaglandin.",
