                        "cot": " \n\nFirst, we need to understand what a congenital cataract is. A congenital cataract is a clouding of the lens of the eye that is present at birth or develops in the first few months of life. \n\nNext, we need to understand what a gene is. A gene is a unit of heredity that is transferred from a parent to a child and is responsible for a specific trait. \n\nNow that we have that information, we can narrow down our choices. PAX-6 is a gene that is responsible for the development of the eye. CRYGS-3 is a gene that is responsible for the production of crystallin, a protein that is found in the eye. LMX-1B is a gene that is responsible for the development of the lens of the eye. PITX-3 is a gene that is responsible for the development of the eye and the brain. \n\nBased on this information, the gene that is most likely to be indicated in a congenital cataract is LMX-1B.",
