                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understanding congenital cataract\nCongenital cataract is a condition where the lens of the eye is cloudy at birth or becomes cloudy during early childhood. It can be caused by genetic mutations or environmental factors.\n\nStep 2: Identifying the genes associated with congenital cataract\nSeveral genes have been identified as being associated with congenital cataract. These include PAX-6, CRYGS-3, LMX-IB, and PITX-3.\n\nStep 3: Analyzing the options\nA) PAX-6: PAX-6 is a gene that plays a critical role in eye development. Mutations in this gene have been associated with a variety of eye disorders, including congenital cataract. Therefore, option A is a possible answer.\n\nB) CRYGS-3: CRYGS-3 is a gene that codes for a protein called gamma-crystallin. Mutations in this gene have been associated with congenital cataract. Therefore, option B is a possible answer.\n\nC) LMX-IB: LMX-IB is a gene that plays a role in the development of the midbrain and hindbrain. While mutations in this gene have been associated with other eye disorders, there is no evidence to suggest that it is commonly indicated in congenital cataract. Therefore, option C is unlikely to be the correct answer.\n\nD) PITX-3: PITX-3 is a gene that plays a role in the development of the eye and other organs. Mutations in this gene have been associated with a variety of eye disorders, including congenital cataract. Therefore, option D is a possible answer.\n\nStep 4: Conclusion\nBased on the analysis, options A, B, and D are all possible answers. However, PAX-6 and CRYGS-3 are more commonly associated with congenital cataract than PITX-3. Therefore, the most likely answer is either A) PAX-6 or B) CRYGS-3.",
