                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Congenital cataract is a clouding of the lens in the eye that is present at birth or develops during the first year of life. It can be caused by genetic mutations or environmental factors such as infections or trauma during pregnancy.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is PAX-6?\n2. What is CRYGS-3?\n3. What is LMX-IB?\n4. What is PITX-3?\n5. Which gene is commonly indicated in congenital cataract?\n\nAnswers:\n1. PAX-6 is a gene that plays a critical role in eye development. It is involved in the formation of the lens, cornea, iris, and retina.\n2. CRYGS-3 is a gene that codes for a protein called gamma-crystallin, which is a major component of the lens. Mutations in this gene can cause cataracts.\n3. LMX-IB is a gene that is involved in the development of the eye and ear. Mutations in this gene have been associated with congenital cataracts.\n4. PITX-3 is a gene that is involved in the development of the eye and other organs. Mutations in this gene can cause a variety of eye disorders, including cataracts.\n5. The gene commonly indicated in congenital cataract is A) PAX-6.",
