                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking for the gene that is commonly indicated in congenital cataract, which is a condition where a person is born with a clouding of the lens in the eye.\n\nStep 2: Research the genes listed in the answer choices\nTo answer this question, we need to have some knowledge about the genes listed in the answer choices and their association with congenital cataract.\n\nA) PAX-6: PAX-6 is a transcription factor involved in the development of the eye and central nervous system. Mutations in PAX-6 have been associated with various eye disorders, including aniridia, a condition characterized by the absence of the iris.\n\nB) CRYGS-3: CRYGS-3 is not a recognized gene symbol. It may be a typo or an incorrect option.\n\nC) LMX-1B: LMX-1B is a transcription factor involved in limb and central nervous system development. Mutations in LMX-1B have been associated with nail-patella syndrome, a condition characterized by abnormalities in the nails, kneecaps, and elbows.\n\nD) PITX-3: PITX-3 is a transcription factor involved in the development of the eye, specifically the lens. Mutations in PITX-3 have been associated with congenital cataracts and anterior segment mesenchymal dysgenesis, a condition characterized by abnormal development of the front part of the eye.\n\nStep 3: Determine the correct answer\nBased on the information gathered in Step 2, the gene commonly indicated in congenital cataract is PITX-3.\n\nAnswer: D) PITX-3",
