                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To determine the correct answer, we need to research each of these genes and their association with congenital cataracts.\n\nA) PAX-6: PAX-6 is a transcription factor involved in the development of the eye, brain, and other organs. Mutations in PAX-6 have been associated with various eye disorders, including aniridia, Peters anomaly, and foveal hypoplasia. However, it is not specifically associated with congenital cataracts.\n\nB) CRYGS-3: I could not find any information on a gene called CRYGS-3. It is possible that this is a typo or an incorrect gene name.\n\nC) LMX- IB: LMX1B is a transcription factor involved in limb and eye development. Mutations in LMX1B have been associated with Nail-Patella Syndrome, which can include ocular abnormalities such as glaucoma and cataracts. However, it is not specifically associated with congenital cataracts.\n\nD) PITX-3: PITX-3 is a transcription factor involved in the development of the eye, specifically the lens. Mutations in PITX-3 have been directly associated with congenital cataracts, as well as other ocular abnormalities such as anterior segment mesenchymal dysgenesis and posterior polar cataracts.\n\nBased on this information, the gene most commonly indicated in congenital cataracts is:\n\nD) PITX-3",
