                    {
                        "id": "06a7679a-29e5-414e-89aa-826c6521c081",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to have a clear understanding of congenital cataracts and the role of genes in their development. Congenital cataracts are a type of cataract that is present at birth or develops during early childhood. They are caused by genetic mutations or other factors that affect the development of the eye's lens. The lens is responsible for focusing light on the retina, and when it becomes cloudy due to a cataract, vision is impaired.\n\nNow, let's gather some helpful knowledge and important additional facts about the genes listed in the question:\n\nA) PAX-6: PAX-6 is a transcription factor that plays a crucial role in the development of the eye and other sensory organs. Mutations in the PAX-6 gene have been associated with various eye disorders, including aniridia, a condition characterized by the absence of the iris.\n\nB) CRYGS-3: CRYGS-3 is not a recognized gene symbol. However, CRYGS is a gene that encodes a protein called gamma-S crystallin, which is a component of the eye lens. Mutations in the CRYGS gene have been associated with congenital cataracts.\n\nC) LMX-1B: LMX1B is a transcription factor that plays a role in the development of the limbs, kidneys, and eyes. Mutations in the LMX1B gene have been associated with Nail-Patella Syndrome, a condition characterized by abnormalities in the nails, kneecaps, and elbows, as well as glaucoma.\n\nD) PITX-3: PITX-3 is a transcription factor that plays a role in the development of the eye, particularly the lens and the anterior segment. Mutations in the PITX-3 gene have been associated with congenital cataracts and anterior segment dysgenesis, a group of disorders affecting the development of the front part of the eye.\n\nNow, let's generate sub-questions to help us answer the original question:\n\n1) Which of the listed genes is most commonly associated with congenital cataracts?\n2) Are there any other genes that are more commonly associated with congenital cataracts than the ones listed?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1:\nBased on the information gathered, PITX-3 (option D) is the gene most commonly associated with congenital cataracts among the listed genes.\n\nAnswering sub-question 2:\nAlthough PITX-3 is a gene commonly associated with congenital cataracts,",
                        "answers": [
