                    {
                        "id": "6623842d-a419-423a-b4f5-6f34290bfdbe",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-10",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking for the best guide for managing resuscitation. Resuscitation is the process of restoring normal physiological functions in a person who is critically ill or injured. The options provided are different physiological parameters that can be used to guide the management of resuscitation.\n\nStep 2: Define the terms\nA) CVP: Central Venous Pressure - the pressure of blood in the thoracic vena cava, near the right atrium of the heart.\nB) Urine output: The amount of urine produced by the kidneys and excreted from the body.\nC) Blood pressure: The pressure of the blood in the circulatory system, often measured for diagnosis since it is closely related to the force and rate of the heartbeat and the diameter and elasticity of the arterial walls.\nD) Saturation of Oxygen: The percentage of hemoglobin binding sites in the bloodstream occupied by oxygen.\n\nStep 3: Evaluate the options\nA) CVP: While CVP can be a useful parameter in some cases, it is invasive and not always the best guide for resuscitation management.\nB) Urine output: Urine output can be an indicator of adequate perfusion and hydration, but it is not the most direct or immediate parameter for guiding resuscitation.\nC) Blood pressure: Blood pressure is an important parameter to monitor during resuscitation, as it provides information about the perfusion of vital organs and the effectiveness of the resuscitation efforts. However, it may not be the most specific indicator of tissue oxygenation.\nD) Saturation of Oxygen: Oxygen saturation is a direct measure of the percentage of hemoglobin binding sites in the bloodstream occupied by oxygen. It is a crucial parameter to monitor during resuscitation, as it provides information about the oxygenation of tissues and the effectiveness of resuscitation efforts.\n\nStep 4: Choose the best answer\nBased on the evaluation of the options, the best guide for the management of resuscitation is:\n\nD) Saturation of Oxygen",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "d2aff3a0-300c-4ed8-85f4-2476956a85c7",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
