                    {
                        "id": "d7fb3efb-7e06-4518-95ab-fd7dc2399033",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To determine the best guide for the management of resuscitation, let's analyze each option in detail:\n\nA) CVP (Central Venous Pressure): CVP is the pressure of blood in the thoracic vena cava, near the right atrium of the heart. It is an important measurement in assessing the volume status and cardiac function of a patient. However, it is an invasive procedure and may not be the best initial guide for resuscitation management.\n\nB) Urine output: Monitoring urine output is a non-invasive method to assess kidney function and fluid balance in the body. While it can provide useful information about the patient's overall condition, it may not be the most accurate or immediate indicator of resuscitation success.\n\nC) Blood pressure: Blood pressure is a vital sign that measures the force of blood against the walls of the arteries. It is an essential parameter to monitor during resuscitation, as it can provide information about the patient's circulatory status and the effectiveness of resuscitation efforts. However, it may not be the only factor to consider in managing resuscitation.\n\nD) Saturation of Oxygen (SpO2): Oxygen saturation is a measure of the amount of oxygen bound to hemoglobin in the blood. It is a crucial parameter to monitor during resuscitation, as it indicates the effectiveness of oxygen delivery to the tissues. Maintaining adequate oxygen saturation is essential for the survival of the patient during resuscitation.\n\nAfter analyzing each option, it is clear that all of these parameters are important in the management of resuscitation. However, if we have to choose the best guide, it would be D) Saturation of Oxygen (SpO2). This is because oxygen saturation is a critical parameter that directly reflects the effectiveness of resuscitation efforts in delivering oxygen to the tissues, which is essential for the patient's survival. Additionally, it is a non-invasive measurement that can be easily and continuously monitored during resuscitation.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "e055571a-2f59-4147-8575-217bf3de3d0e",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Saturation of Oxygen (SpO2).",
                                "answer_from_choices": "",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
