                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question to ensure we understand it correctly: What is the best guide for managing resuscitation?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step, spelling out our thoughts and reasoning so anyone can verify them:\n\nA) CVP (Central Venous Pressure) - This is a measurement of the pressure in the central veins, which can help assess the patient's fluid status and guide fluid management. It can be useful in resuscitation, but it is not the only factor to consider.\n\nB) Urine output - Monitoring urine output can help assess kidney function and overall fluid balance in the body. While it can be an important factor in resuscitation, it is not the sole determinant of successful resuscitation.\n\nC) Blood pressure - Blood pressure is a vital sign that can help determine the effectiveness of resuscitation efforts. Maintaining adequate blood pressure is crucial for perfusion of vital organs. However, relying solely on blood pressure may not provide a complete picture of the patient's condition during resuscitation.\n\nD) Saturation of Oxygen - Oxygen saturation is a measure of the amount of oxygen being carried by hemoglobin in the blood. Ensuring adequate oxygenation is essential during resuscitation, but it is not the only factor to consider.\n\nConsidering all these factors, it is difficult to pinpoint one single best guide for the management of resuscitation, as each of these factors plays a crucial role in the process. However, if we had to choose one, blood pressure (C) is likely the most important factor to monitor during resuscitation, as it is a vital sign that directly reflects the effectiveness of resuscitation efforts and the perfusion of vital organs.",
                        "answers": [
