                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Answer: C) Geological\n\nCritique: The answer is too vague and does not provide any context or explanation as to why geological disasters cause the maximum deaths. It also does not consider the fact that different types of natural disasters may have varying impacts on human populations depending on the specific event and location.\n\nReconsidering other options:\n\nA) Hydrological disasters, such as floods and tsunamis, can cause significant loss of life, especially in densely populated areas. However, the death toll from these events can vary greatly depending on the specific circumstances.\n\nB) Meteorological disasters, such as hurricanes, tornadoes, and heatwaves, can also cause a large number of fatalities. However, the death toll from these events is often lower than that of geological disasters, as they are typically more predictable and allow for better preparation and evacuation measures.\n\nC) Geological disasters, such as earthquakes and volcanic eruptions, can cause massive loss of life due to their sudden onset and the widespread destruction they can cause. Earthquakes, in particular, have been responsible for some of the deadliest natural disasters in history, such as the 2010 Haiti earthquake and the 2004 Indian Ocean earthquake and tsunami.\n\nD) Fires, while they can be deadly, are generally not considered on the same scale as the other natural disasters listed. Wildfires and urban fires can cause significant loss of life and property, but their death tolls are typically lower than those of geological, hydrological, and meteorological disasters.\n\nFinal answer: C) Geological disasters cause the maximum deaths among the given options, primarily due to their sudden onset and potential for widespread destruction.",
