                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: High risk infants are those who have a greater chance of developing health problems or complications compared to other infants.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What factors contribute to an infant being considered high risk?\n2. How does each of the options listed affect an infant's risk level?\n\nAnswer:\n1. Factors that contribute to an infant being considered high risk include premature birth, low birth weight, congenital abnormalities, maternal health conditions, and environmental factors.\n2. A) Working mother: While a working mother may experience stress and fatigue during pregnancy, this alone does not necessarily make the infant high risk.\nB) Antenatal preeclampsia: This condition, characterized by high blood pressure and protein in the urine, can lead to premature birth and low birth weight, making the infant high risk.\nC) Third child: The number of children a mother has does not directly affect an infant's risk level.\nD) Twins: Multiple births, including twins, are considered high risk due to the increased likelihood of premature birth and low birth weight. Therefore, twins are considered high risk infants.",
