                        "cot": "\n\nThe answer is C) Stratified random sampling.\n\nHere's why:\n\nSystematic random sampling involves selecting every nth individual from a list or population, which may not take into account potential factors that could affect the results.\n\nSimple random sampling involves randomly selecting individuals from a list or population, which also may not take into account potential factors that could affect the results.\n\nCluster sampling involves dividing a population into clusters and randomly selecting clusters to sample from, which may not take into account individual factors that could affect the results.\n\nStratified random sampling, on the other hand, involves dividing a population into subgroups (or strata) based on potential factors that could affect the results, and then randomly selecting individuals from each subgroup. This ensures that the sample is representative of the population with respect to those potential factors. Therefore, stratified random sampling is the method used for selecting patients with respect to potential factors that will affect the results.",
                        "answers": [
