                        "cot": "\nStudent: What is the difference between systematic random sampling and simple random sampling?\n\nProfessor: Systematic random sampling is a method of selecting a sample from a larger population by randomly selecting a starting point and then selecting every nth element from the population. Simple random sampling is a method of selecting a sample from a larger population by randomly selecting elements from the population with equal probability. \n\nStudent: What is stratified random sampling?\n\nProfessor: Stratified random sampling is a method of selecting a sample from a larger population by dividing the population into subgroups or strata and then randomly selecting elements from each stratum. This ensures that the sample is representative of the population.\n\nStudent: What is cluster sampling?\n\nProfessor: Cluster sampling is a method of selecting a sample from a larger population by randomly selecting clusters or groups of elements from the population. This is useful when it is difficult to obtain a complete list of the population.",
