                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The Human Development Index (HDI) is a measure of a country's overall development, taking into account factors such as life expectancy, education, and income. The HDI is calculated by the United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) and is used to rank countries based on their level of human development.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the Human Development Index (HDI)?\n2. What factors are taken into account when calculating the HDI?\n3. How is the HDI calculated?\n4. What weightage is given to adult literacy and gross enrolment in the HDI calculation?\n\nAnswer:\n1. The Human Development Index (HDI) is a measure of a country's overall development, taking into account factors such as life expectancy, education, and income.\n2. The factors taken into account when calculating the HDI are life expectancy, education, and income.\n3. The HDI is calculated by taking the geometric mean of the normalized indices for each of the three factors (life expectancy, education, and income).\n4. According to the given statements, 1/3rd weightage is given to adult literacy and 2/3rd weightage is given to gross enrolment in the HDI calculation. Additionally, the statement A) clarifies that only secondary education is considered in gross enrolment, not primary education. The statement D) is false, as a country's achievement is not computed solely on the basis of adult literacy, but rather on a combination of factors including education, income, and life expectancy. Therefore, the answer is D) Country's achievement is computed on the basis of adult literacy.",
