                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is a side-effect of valproate that is specific to a certain gender?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- Valproate is a medication used to treat various conditions such as epilepsy, bipolar disorder, and migraines.\n- A side-effect is an unintended consequence of taking a medication.\n- Gender-specific means that the side-effect only affects one gender.\n- Polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS) is a condition that affects the ovaries and is more common in women. Therefore, it could be a gender-specific side-effect of valproate.\n- Alopecia is a medical term for hair loss, which can affect both men and women. Therefore, it is not a gender-specific side-effect of valproate.\n- Weight loss can occur as a side-effect of valproate, but it is not specific to one gender.\n- Tremor is a medical term for shaking or trembling, which can affect both men and women. Therefore, it is not a gender-specific side-effect of valproate.\n\nAnswer: A) Polycystic ovarian syndrome is a gender-specific side-effect of valproate.",
