                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Valproate is a medication commonly used to treat epilepsy, bipolar disorder, and migraines. It works by increasing the levels of a neurotransmitter called GABA in the brain.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS)?\n2. How does valproate affect hormones in the body?\n3. What are the common side effects of valproate?\n4. Which of the given options are gender-specific?\n\nAnswering sub-questions:\n1. PCOS is a hormonal disorder that affects women of reproductive age. It is characterized by the growth of small cysts on the ovaries, which can cause irregular periods, infertility, and other symptoms.\n2. Valproate can increase the levels of androgens (male hormones) in the body, which can disrupt the balance of hormones in women and lead to PCOS.\n3. Common side effects of valproate include drowsiness, dizziness, nausea, vomiting, and tremor.\n4. The only gender-specific option given is A) Polycystic ovarian syndrome.\n\nAnswer:\nA) Polycystic ovarian syndrome is a gender-specific side-effect of valproate. Valproate can increase the levels of androgens in the body, which can disrupt the balance of hormones in women and lead to PCOS.",
