                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question to ensure we understand it correctly: An 8-year-old child experienced trauma and had their central incisor knocked out 20 minutes ago, which became contaminated with debris. The child goes to a dental clinic with the avulsed tooth. What is the appropriate treatment?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step, spelling out our thoughts and reasoning so anyone can verify them.\n\nA) Clean root surface with saline and reimplant: This option involves cleaning the root surface with saline solution and then reimplanting the tooth. This may help remove debris, but it might not be enough to ensure proper healing and reattachment.\n\nB) Clean and curette root surface and reimplant: This option involves cleaning the root surface and using a curette (a surgical instrument) to remove any remaining debris or damaged tissue before reimplanting the tooth. This could provide a better chance of successful reattachment.\n\nC) Clean root surface, do RCT (root canal treatment), and reimplant: This option involves cleaning the root surface, performing a root canal treatment to remove any damaged or infected pulp, and then reimplanting the tooth. However, performing a root canal treatment on a recently avulsed tooth may not be necessary or appropriate, especially for a young child.\n\nD) Scrubbing of root surface and reimplant: This option involves scrubbing the root surface to remove debris and then reimplanting the tooth. This might be too aggressive and could potentially damage the tooth further.\n\nBased on our analysis, the best treatment option for the 8-year-old child with an avulsed central incisor contaminated with debris is:\n\nB) Clean and curette root surface and reimplant. This option provides thorough cleaning and removal of debris, which can increase the chances of successful reattachment and healing.",
