                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to consider the following important facts and additional knowledge:\n\n1. An avulsed tooth is a tooth that has been completely knocked out of its socket.\n2. Time is a critical factor in the success of reimplantation. The sooner the tooth is reimplanted, the better the chances of success.\n3. Debris and contamination can lead to infection and complications if not properly managed.\n4. The central incisor is a vital tooth, meaning it has a living pulp (nerve) inside.\n\nBased on these facts, we can generate the following sub-questions:\n\n1. What is the best way to clean a contaminated avulsed tooth?\n2. Is it necessary to perform root canal treatment (RCT) before reimplantation in this case?\n3. What is the most appropriate treatment option for this 8-year-old child?\n\nSub-question 1: What is the best way to clean a contaminated avulsed tooth?\nAnswer: The best way to clean a contaminated avulsed tooth is to gently rinse it with saline solution to remove debris without damaging the root surface or the periodontal ligament cells.\n\nSub-question 2: Is it necessary to perform root canal treatment (RCT) before reimplantation in this case?\nAnswer: Performing RCT before reimplantation is not necessary in this case, as the tooth is a vital tooth and the pulp may still be alive. Moreover, the child is only 8 years old, and the tooth's root development may not be complete. RCT can be considered later if the tooth does not revascularize or if signs of infection develop.\n\nSub-question 3: What is the most appropriate treatment option for this 8-year-old child?\nAnswer: Based on the answers to the sub-questions, the most appropriate treatment option for this 8-year-old child is to clean the root surface with saline and reimplant the tooth (Option A). This approach will remove debris and contamination while preserving the vitality of the tooth and the periodontal ligament cells, increasing the chances of successful reimplantation.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "0eb056f5-8c3e-4b8d-a0e7-a913581662e3",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Clean root surface with saline and reimplant.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
