                        "cot": "\n\nMetformin causes lactic acidosis by inhibiting the liver's ability to break down lactate. Therefore, any condition that impairs the liver's function or increases lactate production can increase the risk of lactic acidosis. \n\nA) Advanced age: This is a risk factor for lactic acidosis because aging can impair liver function. Therefore, this increases the risk of lactic acidosis. \n\nB) Smoking: Smoking does not directly affect liver function or lactate production. Therefore, it does not increase the risk of lactic acidosis. \n\nC) Liver dysfunction: This is a risk factor for lactic acidosis because it impairs the liver's ability to break down lactate. Therefore, this increases the risk of lactic acidosis. \n\nD) Renal failure: Renal failure can increase the risk of lactic acidosis because it impairs the body's ability to excrete lactate. Therefore, this increases the risk of lactic acidosis. \n\nTherefore, the answer is B) Smoking.",
