                        "cot": "To answer this question, we first need to understand what hepatic first-pass metabolism is and how it affects drug administration.\n\nHepatic first-pass metabolism refers to the process by which a drug is metabolized in the liver before it reaches the systemic circulation. This can significantly affect the bioavailability of a drug, as a large portion of the drug may be metabolized and inactivated before it reaches its target site in the body.\n\nNow, let's examine each of the drug administration routes listed in the question to determine which one does not bypass hepatic first-pass metabolism:\n\nA) Oral: When a drug is administered orally, it is absorbed through the gastrointestinal tract and then transported to the liver via the portal vein. In the liver, the drug undergoes first-pass metabolism before entering the systemic circulation. Therefore, hepatic first-pass metabolism is not bypassed with oral administration.\n\nB) Intravenous: Intravenous administration involves injecting the drug directly into the bloodstream. This route bypasses the gastrointestinal tract and liver, allowing the drug to enter the systemic circulation without undergoing first-pass metabolism. Therefore, hepatic first-pass metabolism is bypassed with intravenous administration.\n\nC) Sublingual: Sublingual administration involves placing the drug under the tongue, where it is absorbed directly into the bloodstream through the mucous membranes. This route bypasses the gastrointestinal tract and liver, allowing the drug to enter the systemic circulation without undergoing first-pass metabolism. Therefore, hepatic first-pass metabolism is bypassed with sublingual administration.\n\nD) Intradermal: Intradermal administration involves injecting the drug into the dermis, the layer of skin just below the epidermis. The drug is then absorbed into the bloodstream, bypassing the gastrointestinal tract and liver. Therefore, hepatic first-pass metabolism is bypassed 