                    {
                        "id": "37923acb-9f1a-4849-ad14-5cbbf67c3fd1",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nQ: Which of the following routes of drug administration will not bypass first pass metabolism?\nA) Oral\nB) Intravenous\nC) Sublingual\nD) Intradermal\n\nFirst pass metabolism is when a drug is metabolized by the liver before it enters the general circulation. This means that less of the drug will be available to the body. The following routes of administration will bypass first pass metabolism:\n\nB) Intravenous - The drug is directly injected into the bloodstream and bypasses the liver.\nC) Sublingual - The drug is absorbed through the mucous membranes under the tongue and bypasses the liver.\nD) Intradermal - The drug is injected into the skin and bypasses the liver.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "73f80ed4-3e54-442c-9f4e-e50be8ce246c",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": " A) Oral.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "A",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/04/16 20:56:47",
                        "api_service": "openai",
                        "model": "{'name': 'text-davinci-002', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
                        "comment": "",
                        "annotations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "80e93c42-a19e-4b82-9f62-75446ccf3202",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nHepatic first pass metabolism is the metabolism of a drug by the liver before it reaches the general circulation. The liver is the main site of drug metabolism and the first pass effect is the reduction in bioavailability of a drug due to its metabolism by the liver. The routes of drug administration that bypass the liver and avoid first pass metabolism are intravenous, sublingual, and intradermal. Oral administration does not bypass the liver and first pass metabolism does occur.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "bc768dba-e870-4cdd-94d3-1c938a81f3f6",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
