                    },
                    {
                        "id": "bbb32cbc-c08e-45c3-8541-5bd372ec4f27",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nStudent: What is hepatic first pass metabolism?\n\nProfessor: Hepatic first pass metabolism is a process in which drugs are metabolized by the liver before they reach the systemic circulation.\n\nStudent: How does this affect drug administration?\n\nProfessor: Depending on the route of administration, the drug may or may not be subject to hepatic first pass metabolism. For example, if the drug is administered orally, it will be subject to hepatic first pass metabolism, but if it is administered intravenously, it will bypass the liver and not be subject to hepatic first pass metabolism.\n\nStudent: So, which route of drug administration will bypass hepatic first pass metabolism?\n\nProfessor: Intravenous administration will bypass hepatic first pass metabolism. Oral, sublingual, and intradermal administration will not bypass hepatic first pass metabolism. Therefore, the correct answer is B) Intravenous.",
                        "answers": [
