                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Determine the purpose of an IOL (intraocular lens) and why it is placed in the eye.\n2. Understand the potential complications or issues that may arise with an IOL over time.\n3. Research the recommended timeline for IOL replacement or removal.\n4. Consider the age and health of the patient in question.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. An IOL is typically placed in the eye during cataract surgery to replace the natural lens that has become cloudy. It helps to improve vision and reduce the need for glasses or contacts.\n2. One potential issue that may arise with an IOL over time is posterior capsule ossification, which can cause vision problems and may require removal of the IOL.\n3. According to the American Academy of Ophthalmology, IOLs do not typically need to be replaced unless there is a problem or complication. However, in cases of posterior capsule ossification, the IOL may need to be removed.\n4. The age and health of the patient may also be factors in determining whether or not to remove or replace the IOL. If the patient is experiencing vision problems or discomfort, it may be necessary to remove or replace the IOL regardless of the timeline.\n\nBased on this information, the correct answer is A) IOL should be removed in case of Posterior Capsule ossification.",
