                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Understand what an IOL is and why it is placed in the eye.\n2. Research the potential complications or issues that may arise after 10 years of having an IOL.\n3. Evaluate each answer option and determine which one aligns with the research findings.\n\nAnswer:\n1. An IOL (intraocular lens) is a small artificial lens that is placed in the eye during cataract surgery to replace the natural lens that has become cloudy. It helps to improve vision and reduce the need for glasses or contact lenses.\n2. After 10 years, there may be complications such as posterior capsule ossification, which is the hardening of the capsule that holds the IOL in place. This can cause vision problems and may require the IOL to be removed or replaced.\n3. Evaluating each answer option:\nA) IOL should be removed in case of Posterior Capsule ossification - This aligns with the potential complication mentioned above.\nB) Never be removed - This is not a safe or accurate answer as complications may arise and require removal or replacement.\nC) Remove when presbyopia sets in - Presbyopia is a natural age-related condition that affects near vision, and is not necessarily related to the IOL. This answer is not relevant to the question.\nD) Should be changed after 10 years - This answer is not specific enough and does not address the potential complications that may arise.\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) IOL should be removed in case of Posterior Capsule ossification.",
