                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the scenario\nAn IOL (Intraocular Lens) has been placed in a young male. The question is what should be done after 10 years.\n\nStep 2: Analyze the options\nA) IOL should be removed in case of Posterior Capsule ossification - Posterior Capsule ossification is a condition where the capsule behind the IOL becomes cloudy, causing vision problems. In such cases, the IOL may need to be removed. However, the option only mentions this condition and does not provide a comprehensive answer to the question.\n\nB) Never be removed - This option is too absolute and does not consider any potential issues that may arise after 10 years.\n\nC) Remove when presbyopia sets in - Presbyopia is a condition where the eye loses its ability to focus on nearby objects. While this may be a reason to remove the IOL, it is not the only reason and may not be applicable to all cases.\n\nD) Should be changed after 10 years - This option seems to be the most reasonable as it acknowledges that the IOL may need to be changed after a certain period. However, it does not provide any specific reason for why the IOL should be changed.\n\nStep 3: Choose the best option\nBased on the analysis, option D seems to be the most reasonable answer. However, it is important to note that the decision to change or remove the IOL should be made by a qualified ophthalmologist based on the individual's specific circumstances and needs.",
