                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking about the appropriate action to take after 10 years of having an intraocular lens (IOL) placed in a young male.\n\nStep 2: Analyze the options\nA) IOL should be removed in case of Posterior Capsule ossification: This option suggests that the IOL should be removed if there is posterior capsule ossification. However, this is not a time-based action and would only be necessary if ossification occurs.\nB) Never be removed: This option suggests that the IOL should never be removed, regardless of any complications or changes in vision.\nC) Remove when presbyopia sets in: This option suggests that the IOL should be removed when the patient develops presbyopia, which is an age-related condition that affects the ability to focus on near objects. This is not necessarily related to the 10-year timeframe.\nD) Should be changed after 10 years: This option suggests that the IOL should be replaced after 10 years, regardless of any complications or changes in vision.\n\nStep 3: Evaluate the options\nOption A is not necessarily related to the 10-year timeframe and would only be necessary if ossification occurs. Option B is too general and does not take into account any potential complications or changes in vision. Option C is not necessarily related to the 10-year timeframe and is dependent on the development of presbyopia. Option D suggests a time-based action but does not consider the individual patient's needs or complications.\n\nStep 4: Choose the best answer\nBased on the analysis, none of the options provided are the perfect answer. However, option A is the closest to being correct, as it addresses a potential complication that may require the removal of the IOL. It is important to note that the decision to remove or replace an IOL should be based on the individual patient's needs and any complications that may arise, rather than a specific timeframe.",
