                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The original question is asking about the appropriate course of action for an intraocular lens (IOL) implanted in a young male after 10 years. An intraocular lens is an artificial lens that is implanted in the eye to replace the natural lens, usually during cataract surgery. \n\nHelpful knowledge and important additional facts:\n1. IOLs are designed to be a permanent replacement for the natural lens.\n2. Posterior capsule opacification (PCO) is a common complication after cataract surgery, where the lens capsule thickens and becomes cloudy, affecting vision.\n3. Presbyopia is an age-related condition where the eye's lens loses its ability to focus on near objects.\n4. IOLs are made of durable materials and are not expected to degrade or need replacement over time.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. How often does posterior capsule opacification occur after cataract surgery?\n2. What is the typical treatment for posterior capsule opacification?\n3. Can an IOL be adjusted or replaced to address presbyopia?\n4. Are there any reasons to remove or replace an IOL after 10 years?\n\nAnswers to sub-questions:\n1. Posterior capsule opacification occurs in about 20-50% of patients within 2-5 years after cataract surgery.\n2. The typical treatment for posterior capsule opacification is a simple, low-risk laser procedure called YAG laser capsulotomy, which creates an opening in the cloudy capsule to restore clear vision. This procedure does not require the removal or replacement of the IOL.\n3. Some IOLs are designed to address presbyopia, but if a patient with an IOL develops presbyopia, they may need additional corrective measures such as glasses or contact lenses. In some cases, an IOL may be replaced with a multifocal or accommodating IOL to address presbyopia, but this is not a common practice.\n4. There are no standard reasons to remove or replace an IOL after 10 years, as they are designed to be a permanent solution. However, in rare cases, complications or changes in the eye may necessitate the removal or replacement of an IOL.\n\nAnswer to the original question:\nB) Never be removed. An IOL is designed to be a permanent replacement for the natural lens, and there is no standard reason to remove or replace it after 10 years. Posterior capsule opacification can be treated with a laser procedure without removing the IOL, and pres",
                        "answers": [
