                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) IOL should be removed in case of Posterior Capsule ossification: This can be a possible reason for removing or replacing the IOL, but it is not a definite action to be taken after 10 years.\n\nB) Never be removed: This is not entirely true, as there can be situations where the IOL needs to be removed or replaced, such as complications or changes in vision.\n\nC) Remove when presbyopia sets in: Presbyopia is a natural aging process of the eye and is not directly related to the IOL. Therefore, this is not a valid reason to remove the IOL after 10 years.\n\nD) Should be changed after 10 years: There is no specific time frame for changing an IOL. It depends on the individual's eye health and any complications that may arise.\n\nIn conclusion, there is no specific action that should be taken after 10 years for an IOL placed in a young male. The decision to remove or replace the IOL depends on the individual's eye health and any complications that may arise.",
