                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The process by which the amount of a drug in the body decreases after administration, but before entering the systemic circulation is called:\n\nHelpful knowledge and important additional facts:\n- Systemic circulation refers to the part of the circulatory system where blood is transported to and from the body, excluding the lungs.\n- The process in question occurs after administration, meaning the drug has been introduced into the body but has not yet reached the bloodstream.\n- The process results in a decrease in the amount of the drug before it enters systemic circulation.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) What is excretion, and does it occur before a drug enters systemic circulation?\n2) What is the first pass effect, and does it occur before a drug enters systemic circulation?\n3) What is first order elimination, and does it occur before a drug enters systemic circulation?\n4) What is metabolism, and does it occur before a drug enters systemic circulation?\n\nAnswers to sub-questions:\n1) Excretion is the process of removing waste products and substances, including drugs, from the body. It typically occurs after a drug has entered systemic circulation and been metabolized.\n2) The first pass effect is the process by which a drug's concentration is reduced before it reaches systemic circulation. This occurs when a drug is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and passes through the liver, where it may be metabolized, before entering the bloodstream.\n3) First order elimination refers to the rate at which a drug is removed from the body, which is proportional to its concentration in the bloodstream. This process occurs after a drug has entered systemic circulation.\n4) Metabolism is the process by which the body breaks down substances, including drugs, into different compounds. This can occur before a drug enters systemic circulation, such as during the first pass effect.\n\nAnswer to the original question:\nThe process by which the amount of a drug in the body decreases after administration, but before entering the systemic circulation is called the first pass effect (B).",
