                    {
                        "id": "c5e2f4c0-0649-4dea-8c8e-0cdb8d949567",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding Piaget's preoperational stage. Can you help me figure out which of these options is correct?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's start by discussing what the preoperational stage is. According to Piaget, this stage is when children begin to develop their thinking and reasoning skills. They start to use symbols and language to represent objects and ideas. However, their thinking is still limited and not yet logical. \n\nStudent: Okay, so it's when children start to think and reason, but they're not quite logical yet. How does that help me figure out the correct age range?\n\nProfessor: Great question! Let's go through the options one by one. Option A is \"Birth to 18 months.\" Do you think this could be the preoperational stage?\n\nStudent: Hmm, I don't think so. Babies that young probably aren't thinking and reasoning yet, right?\n\nProfessor: Exactly! At that age, children are in the sensorimotor stage, where they're learning about the world through their senses and motor skills. So, option A is not correct. What about option B, \"12 years onwards\"?\n\nStudent: That seems too old for the preoperational stage. Wouldn't children that age already be thinking logically?\n\nProfessor: You're right! By the age of 12, most children have entered the formal operational stage, where they can think abstractly and logically. So, option B is not correct either. Now, let's look at option C, \"2-6 years.\"\n\nStudent: That sounds more like the age when children would be developing their thinking and reasoning skills, but not quite logically yet.\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct! The preoperational stage occurs between the ages of 2 and 6 years. During this time, children's thinking is still limited by their egocentrism, which means they have difficulty seeing things from another person's perspective. They also struggle with conservation tasks, such as understanding that the amount of liquid remains the same when poured into different containers. So, option C is the correct answer.\n\nStudent: Okay, that makes sense. And I guess that means option D, \"7-12 years,\" is not correct because that's when children start to think more logically?\n\nProfessor: Exactly! Option D represents the concrete operational stage, where children begin to develop logical thinking and can solve conservation tasks. They can also start to understand more complex concepts, such as categorization and cause-and-effect relationships.\n\nStudent: Got",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "c1e4cbf8-d9dc-4904-91e0-23bb29c63642",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) 2-6 years, as that is the age range when children are in the preoperational stage according to Piaget's theory.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
