                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Understand what post-operative vision effects are and why they need to be evaluated.\n2. Identify what PCB is and how it relates to cataract extraction surgery.\n3. Determine what types of surveillance are available for evaluating post-operative vision effects.\n4. Evaluate each option and determine which one is used for post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. Post-operative vision effects refer to any changes in vision that occur after surgery. It is important to evaluate these effects to ensure that the surgery was successful and to identify any potential complications.\n2. PCB stands for posterior chamber intraocular lens (IOL) implantation, which is a type of cataract extraction surgery. It involves replacing the cloudy lens with an artificial lens that is placed in the posterior chamber of the eye.\n3. The three types of surveillance available for evaluating post-operative vision effects are active, sentinel, and passive surveillance. Active surveillance involves actively seeking out cases of post-operative vision effects through regular follow-up appointments and monitoring. Sentinel surveillance involves monitoring a specific group of patients who are at high risk for post-operative vision effects. Passive surveillance involves relying on patients to report any post-operative vision effects on their own.\n4. Based on the information provided, it is most likely that option D, routine checkup of all operated cases, is used for evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB. This involves scheduling regular follow-up appointments with all patients who have undergone the surgery to monitor their vision and identify any potential complications.",
