                    },
                    {
                        "id": "02ac43ef-49b2-4161-861a-1252e7fdf3ae",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To evaluate post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB (Prevention of Blindness), we need to understand the different types of surveillance mentioned in the options and determine which one is most suitable for this specific scenario.\n\nA) Active surveillance: This type of surveillance involves actively seeking out and collecting data on a specific health event or condition. It is usually done for a limited period and in a specific population. Active surveillance is resource-intensive and is typically used for urgent public health issues or when accurate data is needed.\n\nB) Sentinel surveillance: This type of surveillance involves monitoring a selected group of healthcare facilities or providers that are considered representative of the larger population. Sentinel surveillance is used to track trends in specific diseases or conditions and can provide early warning signs of potential outbreaks.\n\nC) Passive surveillance: This type of surveillance relies on healthcare providers or facilities to report cases of a specific health event or condition voluntarily. Passive surveillance is less resource-intensive than active surveillance but may be subject to underreporting and other biases.\n\nD) Routine checkup of all operated cases: This option involves regularly examining all patients who have undergone cataract extraction surgery to assess their post-operative vision effects. This approach ensures that all cases are evaluated, but it may be resource-intensive and time-consuming.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) Which type of surveillance is most appropriate for evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB?\n2) Are the post-operative vision effects an urgent public health issue that requires active surveillance?\n3) Is sentinel surveillance sufficient to provide accurate data on post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery?\n\nAnswers to sub-questions:\n1) The most appropriate type of surveillance for evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB would be one that balances the need for accurate data with resource constraints.\n2) Post-operative vision effects are important to monitor, but they may not be considered an urgent public health issue that requires active surveillance.\n3) Sentinel surveillance may provide useful data on trends in post-operative vision effects, but it may not capture all cases or provide the most accurate data.\n\nBased on the answers to the sub-questions, the most suitable option for evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB is:\n\nD) Routine checkup of all operated cases\n\nThis approach ensures that all cases are evaluated, providing accurate data on post-operative vision effects. Although it may be resource-intensive, it is necessary to monitor the outcomes of cataract extraction surgery and ensure the effectiveness of the PCB program.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "37d1840f-d766-41ba-9cb1-8da7d3cdc73f",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Routine checkup of all operated cases.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
