                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nStudent: What is the intrapleural pressure?\nProfessor: The intrapleural pressure is the pressure in the pleural space. It is the pressure that exists between the visceral pleura, covering the lungs, and the parietal pleura, lining the inside of the chest wall.\nStudent: What is the pleural space?\nProfessor: The pleural space is the potential space between the visceral pleura and the parietal pleura. It is a potential space because it only exists when the lungs are inflated. When the lungs are deflated, the pleural space is nonexistent.\nStudent: What is the difference between the intrapleural pressure and the pleural pressure?\nProfessor: The intrapleural pressure is the pressure in the pleural space. The pleural pressure is the pressure of the pleural space plus the pressure of the lungs.\nStudent: What is the difference between the pleural pressure and the alveolar pressure?\nProfessor: The pleural pressure is the pressure of the pleural space plus the pressure of the lungs. The alveolar pressure is the pressure of the alveoli.\nStudent: What is the difference between the intrapleural pressure and the alveolar pressure?\nProfessor: The intrapleural pressure is the pressure in the pleural space. The alveolar pressure is the pressure of the alveoli. They are two different pressures.",
