                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " Optic atrophy is a condition in which the optic nerve is damaged, leading to a decrease in vision. It is most commonly seen in conditions such as glaucoma, optic neuritis, and ischemic optic neuropathy. Retinitis pigmentosa, methanol poisoning, and CRAO are all conditions that can lead to optic nerve damage and thus optic atrophy. Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy, however, is a condition that affects the choroid, which is the layer of blood vessels that supplies the retina with oxygen and nutrients. It does not affect the optic nerve, and thus optic atrophy is not seen in this condition. The correct answer is D.",
                        "answers": [
