                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Optic atrophy is a condition where the optic nerve becomes damaged, leading to a loss of vision. The question is asking which of the given conditions does not lead to optic atrophy. \n\nA) Retinitis pigmentosa is a genetic disorder that affects the retina, leading to progressive vision loss. It does not directly affect the optic nerve, so it is not associated with optic atrophy. \n\nB) Methanol poisoning can cause optic atrophy due to the toxic effects of methanol on the optic nerve. Therefore, it is not the correct answer. \n\nC) Central retinal aerial occlusion (CRAO) is a blockage of the central retinal artery, which can lead to sudden vision loss. It can also cause optic atrophy due to the lack of blood flow to the optic nerve. Therefore, it is not the correct answer. \n\nD) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy is a condition where abnormal blood vessels grow in the choroid, a layer of tissue in the eye. It can lead to vision loss, but it does not directly affect the optic nerve and is not associated with optic atrophy. Therefore, it is the correct answer. \n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is D) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "0ac030b4-d553-4173-9905-6ce547554b17",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
