                        "cot": "Rephrased question: Which of the following conditions does not present with optic atrophy?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- Optic atrophy is a condition where the optic nerve becomes damaged and loses its function.\n- Retinitis pigmentosa is a genetic disorder that affects the retina, not the optic nerve. Therefore, it is unlikely to cause optic atrophy. \n- Methanol poisoning can cause optic nerve damage and subsequent optic atrophy. Therefore, it is not the correct answer.\n- Central retinal aerial occlusion (CRAO) is a blockage of the central retinal artery, which can lead to ischemia (lack of blood flow) and damage to the optic nerve. Therefore, it can cause optic atrophy.\n- Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy is a type of macular degeneration that affects the choroid, not the optic nerve. Therefore, it is unlikely to cause optic atrophy.\n\nAnswer: A) Retinitis pigmentosa",
