                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Optic atrophy is a condition where the optic nerve becomes damaged, leading to vision loss. It can be caused by a variety of factors, including trauma, inflammation, and certain diseases.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is retinitis pigmentosa?\n2. Can methanol poisoning cause optic atrophy?\n3. What is central retinal aerial occlusion (CRAO)?\n4. What is polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Retinitis pigmentosa is a genetic disorder that affects the retina, causing progressive vision loss. While it can lead to optic nerve damage, it does not typically cause optic atrophy.\n2. Yes, methanol poisoning can cause optic atrophy. Methanol is toxic to the optic nerve and can cause irreversible damage if not treated promptly.\n3. Central retinal aerial occlusion (CRAO) is a blockage of the central retinal artery, which can cause sudden vision loss. While it can lead to optic nerve damage, it does not typically cause optic atrophy.\n4. Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy is a type of macular degeneration that affects the blood vessels in the choroid layer of the eye. While it can lead to vision loss, it does not typically cause optic atrophy. \n\nTherefore, the answer is A) Retinitis pigmentosa.",
