                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To determine which condition does not involve optic atrophy, we need to understand what optic atrophy is and then analyze each condition to see if it involves optic atrophy.\n\nOptic atrophy is the degeneration or damage to the optic nerve, which can lead to vision loss. Now let's analyze each condition:\n\nA) Retinitis pigmentosa: This is a group of genetic disorders that affect the retina's ability to respond to light. It causes a slow loss of vision, eventually leading to blindness. Optic atrophy can be a feature of retinitis pigmentosa as the disease progresses and the optic nerve gets damaged.\n\nB) Methanol poisoning: Methanol poisoning can cause optic atrophy due to the toxic effect of methanol on the optic nerve. Methanol is metabolized to formaldehyde and formic acid, which are toxic to the optic nerve, leading to optic atrophy and vision loss.\n\nC) Central retinal arterial occlusion (CRAO): CRAO is a condition where the blood flow to the retina is blocked, usually by a blood clot. This can cause sudden, painless vision loss in the affected eye. The lack of blood flow to the retina can lead to ischemic damage and eventually optic atrophy if the blood flow is not restored.\n\nD) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy: This is a condition that affects the blood vessels in the choroid, which is the layer of blood vessels and connective tissue between the retina and the sclera. It is characterized by the presence of polypoidal lesions and abnormal blood vessels. Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy primarily affects the choroid and the outer retina, but it does not directly involve the optic nerve.\n\nBased on this analysis, the correct answer is:\n\nD) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy\n\nOptic atrophy is not seen in polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy, as this condition primarily affects the choroid and the outer retina, but not the optic nerve.",
