                    {
                        "id": "ee767680-e290-4dbb-903f-c04eda08e1b3",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Optic atrophy is a condition in which the optic nerve, responsible for transmitting visual information from the retina to the brain, becomes damaged and leads to a loss of vision. It can be caused by various factors, including genetic disorders, inflammation, and exposure to toxins. To determine which of the given options does not result in optic atrophy, we need to understand the pathophysiology of each condition.\n\nA) Retinitis pigmentosa: This is a group of genetic disorders that affect the retina's ability to respond to light. It causes a progressive loss of vision due to the degeneration of photoreceptor cells (rods and cones) in the retina. As the disease progresses, it can lead to optic atrophy.\n\nB) Methanol poisoning: Methanol is a toxic substance that can cause severe metabolic acidosis and neurological complications when ingested. It is metabolized to formaldehyde and formic acid, which can cause damage to the optic nerve, leading to optic atrophy.\n\nC) Central retinal arterial occlusion (CRAO): This condition occurs when the central retinal artery, which supplies blood to the retina, becomes blocked. This blockage deprives the retina of oxygen and nutrients, leading to ischemic damage and eventual cell death. If the blockage is not resolved, it can result in optic atrophy.\n\nD) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy: This is a condition characterized by the presence of abnormal blood vessels in the choroid, the vascular layer of the eye that supplies blood to the retina. These abnormal vessels can leak fluid and blood, leading to swelling and damage to the overlying retinal tissue. However, this condition primarily affects the choroid and outer retina, not the optic nerve.\n\nBased on the information above, optic atrophy is not seen in:\n\nD) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "e261cbf1-5b55-406b-bbb2-23f492958a07",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "D",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
