                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The question is asking for the name of a specific test performed by a medical examiner to assess lower limb weakness in a patient. The examiner places one hand under the patient's heel and asks the patient to raise their other leg against downward resistance.\n\nHelpful knowledge and important additional facts:\n- Lower limb weakness can be caused by various conditions, such as nerve damage, muscle disorders, or spinal issues.\n- Clinical tests are used to assess the function and strength of muscles and nerves in the lower limbs.\n\nTo answer the original question, we need to determine which of the given tests (Hoover test, Waddell's test, O'Donoghue test, or McBride test) involves the described procedure.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) What is the Hoover test?\n2) What is Waddell's test?\n3) What is the O'Donoghue test?\n4) What is the McBride test?\n\n1) The Hoover test is a clinical test used to assess the strength and function of the hip muscles. The examiner places one hand under the patient's heel and asks the patient to raise their other leg against downward resistance. The examiner feels for pressure from the heel of the leg being raised, indicating that the patient is using their hip muscles to perform the action.\n\n2) Waddell's test is a group of tests used to assess if a patient's pain is primarily due to a psychological or emotional issue rather than a physical problem. These tests do not involve the procedure described in the question.\n\n3) The O'Donoghue test is a clinical test used to assess the integrity of the ligaments in the knee joint. The examiner moves the patient's knee through a range of motions while applying resistance. This test does not involve the procedure described in the question.\n\n4) The McBride test is a clinical test used to assess the presence of patellar subluxation or dislocation. The examiner places their thumb on the patient's patella and asks the patient to contract their quadriceps muscle. This test does not involve the procedure described in the question.\n\nBased on the answers to the sub-questions, the test described in the original question is the Hoover test (A).",
