                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nThe first step is to review the symptoms presented by the child. The child has coarse facial features, macroglossia (enlarged tongue), thick lips, copious mucous discharge from the nose, and enlarged liver and spleen. \n\nThe next step is to review the four possible diagnoses and determine which one best fits the symptoms. \n\nA) Hurler's syndrome is a rare genetic disorder that affects the body's ability to break down and recycle certain molecules. Symptoms include coarse facial features, macroglossia, thick lips, and enlarged liver and spleen. However, copious mucous discharge from the nose is not a symptom of Hurler's syndrome. \n\nB) Beckwith-Weidman syndrome is a rare genetic disorder that affects the body's ability to break down and recycle certain molecules. Symptoms include macroglossia, thick lips, and enlarged liver and spleen. However, coarse facial features and copious mucous discharge from the nose are not symptoms of Beckwith-Weidman syndrome. \n\nC) Hypothyroidism is a condition in which the thyroid gland does not produce enough of certain hormones. Symptoms include coarse facial features, thick lips, and copious mucous discharge from the nose. However, macroglossia and enlarged liver and spleen are not symptoms of hypothyroidism. \n\nD) Proteus syndrome is a rare genetic disorder that affects the body's ability to break down and recycle certain molecules. Symptoms include coarse facial features, macroglossia, thick lips, and enlarged liver and spleen. Copious mucous discharge from the nose is not a symptom of Proteus syndrome. \n\nBased on the symptoms presented, the correct diagnosis is A) Hurler's syndrome.",
