                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain which position a child with tetralogy of fallot uses?\n\nProfessor: Sure, a child with tetralogy of fallot typically uses the squatting position.\n\nStudent: Why is that?\n\nProfessor: Well, tetralogy of fallot is a congenital heart defect that affects the flow of blood to the lungs. Squatting increases the resistance in the legs, which in turn increases the resistance in the systemic circulation. This helps to balance the blood flow between the pulmonary and systemic circulations, which is important for these patients.\n\nStudent: So, why not supine or prone position?\n\nProfessor: Supine and prone positions do not provide the same level of resistance as squatting. In fact, supine position can actually worsen the symptoms of tetralogy of fallot by decreasing the blood flow to the lungs.\n\nStudent: And what about leaning forwards?\n\nProfessor: Leaning forwards can also help to increase the resistance in the legs, but it is not as effective as squatting. Additionally, it can be uncomfortable for the patient to maintain this position for an extended period of time.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand now. Thank you, professor.\n\nProfessor: You're welcome. Always happy to help clarify things.",
