                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding which position a child with tetralogy of Fallot would use. Can you help me work it out?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's go through the options one by one. First, let's discuss what tetralogy of Fallot is. Do you know what it is?\n\nStudent: Yes, it's a congenital heart defect that involves four anatomical abnormalities, which lead to low oxygen levels in the blood.\n\nProfessor: That's correct. Now, let's look at the first option, the supine position. Do you know what that is?\n\nStudent: Yes, it's lying down on the back, facing upwards.\n\nProfessor: Right. Now, would lying down on the back help increase oxygen levels in the blood for a child with tetralogy of Fallot?\n\nStudent: I'm not sure. I don't think so.\n\nProfessor: You're correct. Lying down on the back wouldn't help increase oxygen levels. Now, let's move on to the prone position. Do you know what that is?\n\nStudent: Yes, it's lying down on the stomach, facing downwards.\n\nProfessor: Correct. And would lying down on the stomach help increase oxygen levels in the blood for a child with tetralogy of Fallot?\n\nStudent: I don't think so either.\n\nProfessor: You're right again. Lying down on the stomach wouldn't help increase oxygen levels. Now, let's look at the squatting position. Do you know what that is?\n\nStudent: Yes, it's when you bend your knees and lower your body, keeping your feet flat on the ground.\n\nProfessor: Exactly. Now, think about how squatting might affect blood flow and oxygen levels in the body. Would squatting help increase oxygen levels in the blood for a child with tetralogy of Fallot?\n\nStudent: I think so, because squatting might help increase blood flow to the lungs and improve oxygenation.\n\nProfessor: That's correct! Squatting can help increase blood flow to the lungs and improve oxygenation in children with tetralogy of Fallot. Finally, let's look at the leaning forward position. Do you know what that is?\n\nStudent: Yes, it's when you bend your upper body forward from the waist.\n\nProfessor: Right. And would leaning forward help increase oxygen levels in the blood for a child with tetralogy of Fallot?\n\nStudent: I don't think so.\n\nProfessor: Correct. Leaning forward wouldn't help increase oxygen levels.",
                        "answers": [
