                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " \n\nFirst, let's look at what tetralogy of fallot is. According to the Mayo Clinic, tetralogy of fallot is a \"congenital heart defect that comprises four heart abnormalities.\" These abnormalities can lead to decreased oxygen in the blood and, as a result, cyanosis (a bluish tint to the skin, lips, and fingernails). \n\nNow that we know a little bit more about tetralogy of fallot, let's look at the positions that are listed in the question. \n\nThe supine position is when a person is lying on their back with their face up. This position would not be good for a child with tetralogy of fallot because it can lead to decreased oxygen in the blood. \n\nThe prone position is when a person is lying on their stomach with their face down. This position would not be good for a child with tetralogy of fallot because it can lead to decreased oxygen in the blood. \n\nThe squatting position is when a person is in a low crouch with their knees bent and their weight on their heels. This position would not be good for a child with tetralogy of fallot because it can lead to decreased oxygen in the blood. \n\nThe leaning forwards position is when a person is leaning forwards with their weight on their hands or elbows. This position would not be good for a child with tetralogy of fallot because it can lead to decreased oxygen in the blood. \n\nBased on the information above, the correct answer is A) Supine.",
                        "answers": [
