                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking for the most likely enzyme deficiency in an adolescent male patient who experiences pain in calf muscles during exercise and has excessive glycogen in his muscles.\n\nStep 2: Review the answer choices\nA) Muscle debranching enzyme\nB) Phosphofructokinase I\nC) Glucose 6 phosphatase\nD) Phosphorylase enzyme\n\nStep 3: Analyze the symptoms and relate them to the enzyme deficiencies\n- Pain in calf muscles during exercise: This symptom suggests that the patient's muscles are not able to produce enough energy during exercise, which could be due to a problem in glycogen metabolism.\n- Excessive glycogen in muscles: This symptom indicates that there is a problem in breaking down glycogen to produce energy.\n\nStep 4: Relate the symptoms to the answer choices\nA) Muscle debranching enzyme: This enzyme is involved in glycogen breakdown, but its deficiency would not cause excessive glycogen accumulation in muscles.\nB) Phosphofructokinase I: This enzyme is involved in glycolysis, which is the breakdown of glucose to produce energy. Its deficiency could cause muscle pain during exercise, but it would not cause excessive glycogen accumulation in muscles.\nC) Glucose 6 phosphatase: This enzyme is involved in the final step of glycogenolysis, which is the conversion of glucose-6-phosphate to glucose. Its deficiency would cause excessive glycogen accumulation in the liver, not in muscles.\nD) Phosphorylase enzyme: This enzyme is involved in glycogen breakdown, and its deficiency would cause excessive glycogen accumulation in muscles and muscle pain during exercise.\n\nStep 5: Choose the most likely answer\nBased on the analysis, the most likely enzyme deficiency in this patient is D) Phosphorylase enzyme.",
