                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To determine the most likely enzyme deficiency in this adolescent male patient, we need to consider the symptoms and the findings from the biopsy. The patient is experiencing pain in the calf muscles during exercise, and the biopsy shows an excessive amount of glycogen present in the muscle.\n\nLet's analyze each enzyme deficiency option:\n\nA) Muscle debranching enzyme: A deficiency in this enzyme would lead to a buildup of glycogen with abnormal branching patterns. This condition is known as glycogen storage disease type III (Cori disease). The symptoms include muscle weakness and hypoglycemia. Although there is a buildup of glycogen in the muscles, the primary symptom of pain during exercise does not match this condition.\n\nB) Phosphofructokinase I: A deficiency in this enzyme leads to glycogen storage disease type VII (Tarui disease). This condition is characterized by exercise intolerance, muscle cramps, and pain during exercise due to the inability to break down glycogen in the muscles. This option seems to match the patient's symptoms and biopsy findings.\n\nC) Glucose 6 phosphatase: A deficiency in this enzyme causes glycogen storage disease type I (von Gierke disease). This condition is characterized by hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, and an enlarged liver. The primary symptoms do not match the patient's presentation.\n\nD) Phosphorylase enzyme: A deficiency in this enzyme leads to glycogen storage disease type V (McArdle disease). This condition is characterized by exercise intolerance, muscle cramps, and pain during exercise due to the inability to break down glycogen in the muscles. This option also seems to match the patient's symptoms and biopsy findings.\n\nBoth options B (Phosphofructokinase I deficiency) and D (Phosphorylase enzyme deficiency) present with similar symptoms and findings. However, the excessive amount of glycogen found in the muscle biopsy is more consistent with a deficiency in the Phosphorylase enzyme (Option D), as this enzyme is directly responsible for breaking down glycogen in the muscles. In contrast, Phosphofructokinase I is involved in glycolysis, which is the breakdown of glucose, not glycogen.\n\nTherefore, the most likely enzyme deficiency in this patient is D) Phosphorylase enzyme.",
