                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The positive pivot shift test in the knee is a clinical test used to diagnose instability in the knee joint. It is performed by flexing the knee to 90 degrees and applying a valgus force while internally rotating the tibia. If the tibia subluxates anteriorly, then it is a positive test.\n\nThe positive pivot shift test is typically associated with an injury to the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL). The ACL is responsible for stabilizing the knee joint during rotational movements, and a tear or injury to this ligament can result in a positive pivot shift test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Anterior cruciate ligament. Injuries to the posterior cruciate ligament (A), medial meniscus (C), and lateral meniscus (D) can also cause knee instability, but they are not typically associated with a positive pivot shift test.",
