                    {
                        "id": "795d3a8c-fd13-40aa-a9aa-6d73579e4a45",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The pivot shift test is a clinical examination used to assess knee instability. It involves applying a valgus force to the knee while it is flexed and then extending the knee. A positive pivot shift test indicates that there is an abnormal movement of the tibia relative to the femur during knee extension.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the function of the posterior cruciate ligament (PCL)?\n2. What is the function of the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL)?\n3. What is the function of the medial meniscus?\n4. What is the function of the lateral meniscus?\n5. Which structure is responsible for preventing anterior translation of the tibia on the femur?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The PCL is one of the four major ligaments in the knee joint. It prevents posterior translation of the tibia on the femur.\n2. The ACL is another major ligament in the knee joint. It prevents anterior translation of the tibia on the femur.\n3. The medial meniscus is a C-shaped piece of fibrocartilage that sits on the medial side of the knee joint. It acts as a shock absorber and helps to distribute load across the knee joint.\n4. The lateral meniscus is a similar structure to the medial meniscus, but it sits on the lateral side of the knee joint.\n5. The ACL is responsible for preventing anterior translation of the tibia on the femur.\n\nBased on this information, we can conclude that a positive pivot shift test is most likely due to an injury to the ACL (option B). The PCL and menisci are not directly involved in preventing anterior translation of the tibia on the femur, so an injury to these structures would not cause a positive pivot shift test.",
                        "answers": [
