                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: What is the cause of a positive pivot shift test in the knee?\n\nStep 1: Understand the pivot shift test\nThe pivot shift test is a physical examination performed by a healthcare professional to assess the stability of the knee joint, specifically focusing on the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL).\n\nStep 2: Analyze the answer choices\nA) Posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) - This ligament is responsible for preventing the tibia from sliding too far backward. An injury to the PCL would not cause a positive pivot shift test.\nB) Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) - This ligament is responsible for preventing the tibia from sliding too far forward. An injury to the ACL could cause a positive pivot shift test.\nC) Medial meniscus - This is a piece of cartilage that acts as a cushion between the femur and tibia. An injury to the medial meniscus would not cause a positive pivot shift test.\nD) Lateral meniscus - This is another piece of cartilage that acts as a cushion between the femur and tibia. An injury to the lateral meniscus would not cause a positive pivot shift test.\n\nStep 3: Determine the correct answer\nBased on the analysis of the answer choices, the correct answer is B) Anterior cruciate ligament. A positive pivot shift test in the knee is due to an injury to the anterior cruciate ligament.",
