                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The positive pivot shift test in the knee is an indication of an injury to a specific structure within the knee joint. To determine which structure is affected, we need to understand the anatomy of the knee and the purpose of the pivot shift test.\n\nThe knee joint is a complex structure composed of bones, ligaments, and menisci. The four main ligaments in the knee are the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL), posterior cruciate ligament (PCL), medial collateral ligament (MCL), and lateral collateral ligament (LCL). The menisci are two C-shaped pieces of cartilage that act as shock absorbers between the femur and tibia, and they are called the medial meniscus and lateral meniscus.\n\nThe pivot shift test is a clinical test used to assess the integrity of the ACL. It is performed by applying a valgus force to the knee while the leg is flexed and then extended. A positive pivot shift test occurs when there is a sudden \"shift\" or \"clunk\" as the tibia reduces back into its normal position relative to the femur. This indicates that the ACL is not providing adequate stability to the knee joint.\n\nNow, let's answer the sub-questions to determine which structure is affected by a positive pivot shift test:\n\n1. Does a positive pivot shift test indicate an injury to the posterior cruciate ligament (PCL)?\nNo, the PCL is not primarily responsible for preventing the pivot shift phenomenon. The PCL's main function is to prevent posterior translation of the tibia relative to the femur.\n\n2. Does a positive pivot shift test indicate an injury to the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL)?\nYes, the ACL is the primary stabilizer against the pivot shift phenomenon. An injury to the ACL can result in a positive pivot shift test, as the ligament is not providing adequate stability to the knee joint.\n\n3. Does a positive pivot shift test indicate an injury to the medial meniscus?\nNo, the medial meniscus is not primarily responsible for preventing the pivot shift phenomenon. Its main function is to provide shock absorption and stability to the medial compartment of the knee joint.\n\n4. Does a positive pivot shift test indicate an injury to the lateral meniscus?\nNo, the lateral meniscus is not primarily responsible for preventing the pivot shift phenomenon. Its main function is to provide shock absorption and stability to the lateral compartment of the knee joint.\n\nBased on the information above, a positive pivot shift test in the knee is because of",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "c36c6b04-b9c7-4566-aaf6-47acc1d3f4c1",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Anterior cruciate ligament.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
