                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I am having trouble understanding why a positive pivot shift test in the knee is due to an injury. Can you help me understand this better?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's start by discussing what a pivot shift test is. It's a clinical test used to assess the integrity of the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) in the knee. A positive test indicates that there is an injury to the ACL. Now, let's look at the options given and work through them step by step.\n\nStudent: Okay, so option A is the posterior cruciate ligament. Why is this not the correct answer?\n\nProfessor: The posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) is responsible for preventing the tibia from sliding too far backward. A pivot shift test specifically tests the ACL, which prevents the tibia from sliding too far forward. So, an injury to the PCL would not result in a positive pivot shift test.\n\nStudent: That makes sense. What about option C, the medial meniscus?\n\nProfessor: The medial meniscus is a piece of cartilage that acts as a cushion between the femur and tibia. While an injury to the medial meniscus can cause knee pain and instability, it does not directly affect the ACL or the pivot shift test. So, this is not the correct answer either.\n\nStudent: Alright, and option D is the lateral meniscus. Why is this not the right answer?\n\nProfessor: Similar to the medial meniscus, the lateral meniscus is also a piece of cartilage that provides cushioning between the femur and tibia. An injury to the lateral meniscus can cause pain and instability, but it does not directly affect the ACL or the pivot shift test.\n\nStudent: So, that leaves us with option B, the anterior cruciate ligament. Can you explain why this is the correct answer?\n\nProfessor: Absolutely! As I mentioned earlier, the pivot shift test is specifically designed to assess the integrity of the ACL. A positive pivot shift test indicates that there is an injury to the ACL, which is responsible for preventing the tibia from sliding too far forward. So, a positive pivot shift test in the knee is because of an injury to the anterior cruciate ligament.\n\nStudent: I see! So, the positive pivot shift test is directly related to the ACL, and that's why the correct answer is option B. Thank you for explaining this to me, Professor!\n\nProfessor: You're welcome! I'm glad I could help you",
