                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nStudent: What is the correct answer?\n\nProfessor: Well, let's work through the options. A) Nuclear power does not require decaying vegetation, so that's not the right answer. B) Emitting light beams does not require decaying vegetation either. C) Gas powered motors also do not require decaying vegetation. That leaves us with D) Windmills to power electric grids. Windmills use the energy from the wind to generate electricity, and decaying vegetation is part of the process that enables windmills to do that. So, the correct answer is D. \n\nStudent: So, decaying vegetation is necessary for windmills to generate electricity?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. Decaying vegetation is part of the process that enables windmills to generate electricity.",
