                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding this question about rabbit genetics. Can you help me work through it?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's take it step by step. The question is asking what factor would cause a rabbit to have long ears. Let's look at each option and discuss whether it could be the reason.\n\nStudent: Alright, let's start with option A: rabbits are born with ears.\n\nProfessor: That's true, rabbits are born with ears. But does that necessarily mean they will have long ears?\n\nStudent: I guess not. It just means they have ears, but it doesn't specify the length.\n\nProfessor: Exactly. So we can eliminate option A. Now let's move on to option B: there was a lot of food.\n\nStudent: I don't see how the amount of food would affect the length of a rabbit's ears. Is there a connection?\n\nProfessor: Good observation. The amount of food might affect the overall health and size of the rabbit, but it wouldn't specifically determine the length of its ears. So we can eliminate option B as well.\n\nStudent: Okay, that makes sense. Now let's look at option C: genetic contributors had long ears.\n\nProfessor: This option seems more promising. What do you know about genetics and how traits are passed down from parents to offspring?\n\nStudent: Well, I know that offspring inherit traits from their parents through their genes. So if the parents have long ears, it's more likely that their offspring will also have long ears.\n\nProfessor: That's correct! So option C is a strong possibility. But let's examine option D before we make a final decision: parents were also rabbits.\n\nStudent: I mean, that's true, but it doesn't really have anything to do with the length of the ears, right?\n\nProfessor: Exactly. Just because the parents are rabbits doesn't necessarily mean their offspring will have long ears. So we can eliminate option D.\n\nStudent: So that leaves us with option C: genetic contributors had long ears. That must be the correct answer.\n\nProfessor: Yes, you got it! The length of a rabbit's ears is determined by its genetics, so if the genetic contributors had long ears, it's more likely that the rabbit will also have long ears.\n\nStudent: Thank you, Professor! I understand it much better now.",
