                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: A Punnett square is a diagram used to predict the probability of an offspring inheriting a particular trait from its parents. It is based on the principles of Mendelian genetics.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the trait being considered?\n2. What are the genotypes of the parents?\n3. What are the possible gametes that each parent can produce?\n4. What are the possible combinations of gametes in the offspring?\n5. What is the probability of each possible genotype in the offspring?\n6. What is the probability of each possible phenotype in the offspring?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The trait being considered must be specified in order to use a Punnett square.\n2. The genotypes of the parents must be known or inferred from available information.\n3. The possible gametes that each parent can produce can be determined by using the rules of Mendelian genetics. For example, if the parents are both heterozygous for a particular trait, they can produce gametes with either the dominant or recessive allele.\n4. The possible combinations of gametes in the offspring can be shown in a Punnett square. Each box in the square represents a possible genotype in the offspring.\n5. The probability of each possible genotype in the offspring can be calculated by counting the number of boxes in the Punnett square that correspond to each genotype and dividing by the total number of boxes.\n6. The probability of each possible phenotype in the offspring can be calculated by considering the relationship between the genotype and the phenotype for the trait being considered. For example, if the trait is determined by a single gene with complete dominance, the phenotype of a heterozygous individual will be the same as that of a homozygous dominant individual.",
                        "answers": [
